<title>BRAZIL: Brazil 97/98 soybean exports to rise 58 pct - Safras.</title>
<headline>Brazil 97/98 soybean exports to rise 58 pct - Safras.</headline>
<dateline>RIO DE JANEIRO 1996-12-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>Brazilian soybean exports should grow 58 percent to 5.8 million tonnes in the 1997/98 marketing year (February/January) from this year's 3.6 million tonnes, said independent consultants Safras e Mercado.</p>
<p>Safras revised its forecast up from its earlier estimate of 5.4 million tonnes, saying that the lifting of a state export tax earlier this year should favor soybean exports over sales overseas of soymeal and soyoil.</p>
<p>While excess soybean production was likely to be absorbed by the export market, crushing should remain stable. Meanwhile, higher domestic demand for soymeal and soyoil should compensate for any drop in exports of these products, Safras said.</p>
<p>Safras e Mercado's supply/demand outlook for the 1997/98 marketing year is as follows:  </p>
<p>Quantities are in 1,000 tonnes.</p>
<p> SOYBEANS		 1997/98	 1996/97	  1995/96</p>
<p> Opening stocks	    333	     488	     359</p>
<p> Production	     26,277	  23,495	  25,900</p>
<p> Imports		     800	   1,300	     798</p>
<p> Seeds and losses	1,500	   1,500	   1,450</p>
<p> Exports		   5,800	   3,650	   3,520</p>
<p> Crushings		19,800	  19,800	  21,599</p>
<p> Closing stocks	    310	     333	     488</p>
<p> SOYMEAL		  1997/98	 1996/97	  1995/96</p>
<p> Opening stocks	    297	     547	     468</p>
<p> Production	     15,650	  15,700	  16,946</p>
<p> Imports		     100	     150		15</p>
<p> Internal demand	 5,600	   5,300	   5,137</p>
<p> Exports		  10,150	  10,800	  11,745</p>
<p> Closing stocks	    297	     297	     547</p>
<p> SOYOIL		   1997/98	 1996/97	  1995/96</p>
<p> Opening stocks	    207	     195	     166</p>
<p> Production		3,770	   3,762	   4,074</p>
<p> Imports		     200	     200	     218</p>
<p> Internal demand	 2,750	   2,650	   2,573</p>
<p> Exports		   1,280	   1,300	   1,690</p>
<p> Closing stocks	    147	     207	     195</p>
<p>--Joelle Diderich, Rio de Janeiro newsroom +55 21 507 4151</p>
